      "bodyTr": "National Museum of Korea Director Yu Hong-joon apologized twice during a parliamentary audit yesterday for the controversy surrounding his self-submission and verification of calligraphy by a pro-Japanese collaborator in his personal collection. \"Due to this grave oversight, the public's trust in the museum and the dedication of museum staff collapsed in an instant,\" he said, apologizing to the nation, bereaved families of independence activists, and museum employees. He particularly acknowledged that after the problematic calligraphy was revealed to belong to a pro-Japanese figure rather than an independence activist, and the museum withdrew the work last month and issued an official apology, he had continued to conceal his ownership. \"As an art historian, this was a grave error and oversight,\" he stated.\n\nThe National Museum of Korea's special exhibition \"Our Table\" featuring calligraphy by pro-Japanese collaborator Park Jung-yang was planned under the previous director Kim Jae-hong. After Director Yu took office in July last year, he recommended displaying calligraphy he purchased for 50,000 won at an online auction in 2019, and without any meaningful verification process, the exhibition was swiftly confirmed. Director Yu is a renowned storyteller whose \"Histories of My Cultural Heritage\" series sold over one million copies in the early 1990s. However, he failed to identify the calligraphy as belonging to a pro-Japanese figure, mistakenly believing it was written by independence activist Park Won-geun. His credibility as an art historian has been damaged. Director Yu's 2002 work \"Chusa Kim Jung-hee: A Biography\" (three volumes) was also taken out of print after numerous factual errors were discovered.\n\nErrors in personal publications are the responsibility of the author and publisher. However, mistakes at the nation's premier museum, which attracts over six million visitors annually and ranks among the world's greatest, are inevitably catastrophic. Moreover, considering that provenance—the exhibition and ownership history of artworks—is crucial to determining a work's value, Director Yu's self-recommendation of his own collection is inevitably entangled in conflict of interest concerns.\n\nOnly Director Yu knows what motivated him to push for displaying his personal collection, but he surely understood that in Korean public service culture, a director's self-recommendation carries the weight of an order. Public officials must avoid even the appearance of impropriety. While he apologized using the language of \"error\" and \"oversight,\" many citizens suspect \"ulterior motives.\" Unless measures to restore trust—substantive enough to satisfy the public, including the question of his own position—follow, public interest in the heated National Museum of Korea controversy may cool entirely.",
